 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T19:07+08:00",
 "answer": "This discrepancy happens because your app and the official PSI/API source are using **different systems, formulas, and thresholds** to measure and categorize air quality. \n\nThe difference usually comes down to a few key factors:\n\n### 1. Different Indexes (API/PSI vs. US AQI)\n* **The Official PSI/API:** If you are checking local government data (such as Malaysia's Department of Environment APIMS or Singapore’s NEA), they use their own national indices (Air Pollutant Index or Pollutant Standards Index). These systems have unique mathematical formulas, pollutant concentration brackets, and thresholds for what counts as \"Moderate\" versus \"Unhealthy\". \n* **Your Weather App:** Most third-party smartphone apps (like Apple Weather, Google Weather, IQAir, or AccuWeather) rely on the **US EPA’s Air Quality Index (AQI)**. The US AQI has stricter breakpoints, meaning the exact same amount of pollution that registers as \"Moderate\" on a local government scale can easily flip into \"Unhealthy\" (or \"Unhealthy for Sensitive Groups\") on an international app.\n\n### 2. Different Averaging Periods and Data Updates\n* **Averaging Windows:** Official indices often use a rolling 24-hour average to smooth out sudden spikes. Meanwhile, many commercial apps report real-time or hourly metrics that capture immediate changes. If pollution rolled in heavily over the last few hours, a real-time app will reflect that spike much faster than a 24-hour averaged official score.\n* **Source Variations:** Some apps supplement ground-station data with satellite observations, mathematical modeling, or estimates from low-cost sensors. Satellites measure atmospheric column density rather than ground-level air, which can sometimes skew higher if they misinterpret clouds, haze, or moisture.\n\n### Which one should you trust?\nIf you are trying to make health or travel decisions, **trust your senses and the more cautious reading.** \n\nExperts generally advise that if you step outside and can physically smell smoke, experience throat irritation, or see a heavy haze, the air quality is already affecting you. Regardless of whether an official index labels it \"Moderate,\" it is safer to follow precautions (such as limiting outdoor exercise or wearing a mask) if your app or your own body is telling you the air is unhealthy.",
